findings.body:
The refactor folds the repeated drain loops into `discard_replies_every`, but the condition is inverted. With `!= 0`, the helper drains 10,000 replies on every iteration *except* every 10,000th, while each iteration sends only one command. The first `$rd read` consumes the single pending reply and the second one blocks forever, so the defrag suite hangs on the first non-accurate loop (line 362) before reaching the new test.

::diff{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="70-76"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The base code used `if {$count % 10000 == 0}`. The intended condition is `== 0`.

::suggestion{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="70-76"}
    proc discard_replies_every {rd count frequency discard_num} {
        if {$count % $frequency == 0} {
            for {set k 0} {$k < $discard_num} {incr k} {
                $rd read ; # Discard replies
            }
        }
    }

`streamEntryIsReferenced()` now returns 1 when `s->cgroups_ref` is NULL. A NULL `cgroups_ref` means no consumer group has the entry in its PEL: the rax is only created by `streamLinkCGroupToEntry()` and is only rebuilt by RDB load when the PEL is non-empty (src/rdb.c:2977). Returning 1 marks the entry as referenced, so XADD/XTRIM/XDELEX/XACKDEL with ACKED stop trimming acknowledged entries after a restart or `DEBUG RELOAD`.

The new test in ::file{file="tests/unit/type/stream.tcl"} catches this: after the second `DEBUG RELOAD` the PEL is empty and `cgroups_ref` is NULL, so `XADD ... MAXLEN = 1 ACKED 3-0` cannot trim 1-0 and `XLEN` stays 3 instead of the asserted 2.

::diff{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2707-2709"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Returning 0 is correct here. The `min_cgroup_last_id` check above already returns 1 for entries a group has not read yet; if the entry is at or below the minimum last_id and is absent from the PEL, every group has acknowledged it.

::suggestion{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2708-2708"}
    if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 0;

The comment says "Restore the original active defragmentation setting", but the code hard-codes `1`. `orig_active_defrag` is captured on line 1956 and never used.

`server.active_defrag_enabled` is the storage variable for the `activedefrag` config (src/config.c:3111), so every replica full-sync flush silently turns active defrag on even when the user configured it off. `CONFIG GET activedefrag` then reports `yes`, and serverCron's `activeDefragCycle()` starts running on the replica. The new test enables defrag before `replicaof`, so it cannot detect this.

::diff{file="src/replication.c" lines="1953-1962"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The leftover `orig_active_defrag` also triggers `-Wunused-but-set-variable` under the project's `-Wall -W` flags.

::suggestion{file="src/replication.c" lines="1961-1962"}
    /* Restore the original active defragmentation setting. */
    server.active_defrag_enabled = orig_active_defrag;

The new regression test sets `activedefrag no` at the start, then enables it just before `replicaof`, so it only exercises the enabled path. Nothing asserts that a disabled setting survives the flush — the behavior the fix claims to provide. With the hard-coded restore in ::file{file="src/replication.c"}, a user who disabled defrag would have it silently turned on after every replica sync and no test would fail.

::diff{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="982-986"}

::callout{type="info"}
Add a sibling test that leaves `activedefrag` at its default `no`, loads enough keys to make the flush non-trivial, runs `replicaof` against an empty master, and asserts `CONFIG GET activedefrag` still returns `activedefrag no`.
